The `judge` crash is fixed: an odd-length list envelope now answers `:yin.head/malformed`. A new portable regression test shows that no input shape makes `judge` or `verify` throw. The focused JVM tests and kondo are green.

## What changed and why each entry point is now safe
Only `src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc` changed in code.

- **`judge`:** it now runs `(well-formed? trace)` first and answers `:yin.head/malformed` if that fails. It destructures the envelope only after the shape check passes. The old code destructured first; on the JVM, map-destructuring an odd-length seq throws `IllegalArgumentException`. I confirmed that with the old expression on `(list 1 2 3)`. Every case that already worked gives the same answer, since the shape check was already the first branch.
- **`well-formed?`:** it checks `map?` and the closed key set of the trace before reading the envelope or proof. It then checks `map?` and the key sets of those two before reading any field inside them. A host that decodes a map as something that isn't `map?` gets `false`. The existing `try`/`catch` stays as a backstop.
- **`verify`:** it reads nested values only after `well-formed?` passes, inside its own `try`.
- **`trace`:** it only builds maps from its own arguments, then validates. It is meant to throw on bad input, so I left it alone.
- **`sign.cljc`:** no change was needed.
  - `verify-trace` and `verify-envelope` do no destructuring, only keyword lookups, all inside their existing `try`/`catch`.
  - `sign-trace` reads one keyword and, as a signing call, may throw.

## Commands and outcomes
- `clj -M:test -n yin.vm.linker.head-test -n yin.vm.linker.sign-test`: **13 tests, 220 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.** Last round it was 12 tests and 155 assertions; the new test adds 65.
- `clj -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/head.cljc test/yin/vm/linker/head_test.cljc`: **0 errors, 0 warnings.**
